                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c334d7cf-9c59-34a8-9248-c32bcd5fffd7",
                        "entry_id": "aa2dd663-7b68-3cd8-aedb-680a9a5a499e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Aligner précisément les différentes parties d’un instrument d’optique pour qu’il fonctionne correctement."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b9e86e5f-e1a4-43f8-b03f-efd59b28927e",
                        "source_entry_id": "aa2dd663-7b68-3cd8-aedb-680a9a5a499e",
                        "source_sense_id": "c334d7cf-9c59-34a8-9248-c32bcd5fffd7",
                        "target_lang_word": "collimate",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "collimate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a92bb9ad-b2d5-3cc8-bf77-2f9c6c59b947",
                                        "entry_id": "19e846c3-46a6-3b27-bd65-d75b37d8df1c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To focus into a narrow beam or column; to adjust a focusing device so that it produces a narrow beam."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "619e92b5-d0bf-4cb0-a1d0-80881eee0833",
                                                "sense_id": "a92bb9ad-b2d5-3cc8-bf77-2f9c6c59b947",
                                                "text": "Lead bricks were placed around the radioactive source so that the escaping gamma rays would be limited to a collimated beam rather than filling the lab."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1c65f29b-5690-4ade-b574-19aed0ed0adb",
                                                "sense_id": "a92bb9ad-b2d5-3cc8-bf77-2f9c6c59b947",
                                                "text": "I need to collimate my telescope so that the images are clearer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d6c34fd6-c44c-4f1a-bd97-e9e3c5418529",
                                                "sense_id": "a92bb9ad-b2d5-3cc8-bf77-2f9c6c59b947",
                                                "text": "However, after a bit of experience, the collimating chore can become routine.[…]Of course, if manufacturers made telescopes whose optics did not move around, you would only have to collimate a telescope once."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a1f035d4-1710-4c7d-98aa-5396d6ff251e",
                                                "sense_id": "a92bb9ad-b2d5-3cc8-bf77-2f9c6c59b947",
                                                "text": "Binoculars can be collimated by either eccentric rings on the objective lenses or by tilting the prisms with grub screws (set screws).[…]Always collimate binoculars outdoors, or indoors by looking through an open window."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "707c3dc3-0859-4dee-9c7a-c09f0e5006b3",
                                                "sense_id": "a92bb9ad-b2d5-3cc8-bf77-2f9c6c59b947",
                                                "text": "When calculating the size of the spot created on the retina by the lens of the eye when focusing the energy from a collimated source, the eye is generally considered to be ideal and diffraction limited."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
